  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday morning through next Thursday)\nIssued at 142 PM EST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nOn Sunday, the low that had been over the Southeast will kick up \ninto Mid-Atlantic, with drier air filtering in across Georgia \nthroughout the day bringing pleasant weather conditions. Lingering\nrain showers in the morning across Northeast GA will slowly move \nout, with skies gradually clearing across the area by late \nafternoon. Temperatures will remain warm, with highs around 70\nfor most.\n\nModel guidance remains in generally good agreement on Monday, \nwith a decaying frontal boundary pushing across the area out of \nthe TN Valley. Rain chances look to be 10% or less with this front\ndue to the lack of moisture and it falling apart. Temps will \nremain warm, with morning lows in the 50s and highs in the low 70s.\n\nBy Monday night, guidance starts to have some considerable spread\non the track of a shortwave moving across the Gulf, and another \nwave ejecting out of the Rockies. The track, speed, and strength \nof these systems and how they potentially interact will determine \nthe weather conditions in Georgia from Tuesday through the end of \nnext week. At this time, the general consensus of guidance has \nthese systems starting to introduce rain chances again Tuesday, \npeaking in coverage Wed/Thu, and then a dry/cool airmass moving in\nThu/Fri. The range of potential outcomes with the upcoming setup \nis very large, with some solutions having the shortwaves move \nthrough quickly with little rainfall (0.1-0.25\" or less), while \nothers on the high end have a stronger system developing with a \nheavy rain threat for our area. It is too early to lock on to any \nof these scenarios, so the bottom line is that there is likely to \nbe rain chances in the area Tue-Thu next week, with significant \nuncertainty as to how much and where. Confidence in the forecast \nwill increase in the coming days, so stay tuned!",
